WEBVTT

00:00:00.085 --> 00:00:04.030
FIPS 204 is the Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Standard.

00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:08.780
The standardized algorithm name is ML-DSA, associated with the Dilithium family.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.505
Digital signatures provide authentication, integrity,

00:00:11.505 --> 00:00:14.160
and non-repudiation at a practical level.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.800
They help prove that a message, artifact, certificate, update,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.465
or transaction was signed by the holder of a private key and was not changed after

00:00:20.465 --> 00:00:21.360
signing.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.800
Signature migration can be operationally harder than key establishment migration because

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.717
signatures appear in long-lived trust structures.

00:00:27.717 --> 00:00:31.815
Certificates, code signing, firmware signing, document signing, package repositories,

00:00:31.815 --> 00:00:33.735
identity systems, secure boot chains,

00:00:33.735 --> 00:00:38.280
and update pipelines may all depend on signature formats and verification behavior.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.055
Some signed artifacts need to remain verifiable for years.

00:00:41.055 --> 00:00:44.770
ML-DSA gives organizations a standardized post-quantum signature option,

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.990
but adoption still requires engineering work.

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:52.280
Certificate formats, protocol support, hardware security modules, key management systems,

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.490
build pipelines, verification libraries, device firmware,

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.160
and policy controls may all need updates.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.540
Teams should avoid assuming that a new algorithm name automatically creates an end-to-end

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.040
migration path.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.200
The practical migration question is where trust depends on signatures and how long that

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.186
trust must last.

00:01:07.186 --> 00:01:10.930
A short-lived internal token has a different risk profile from firmware that may be

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.644
installed on devices for years.

00:01:12.644 --> 00:01:16.855
A code signing key has different operational requirements from a service certificate.

00:01:16.855 --> 00:01:20.440
Understanding those differences helps teams prioritize signature migration instead of

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:21.760
treating every use case the same.
